 "reflection": {
  "text": "The call was correct because quantitative atlas matching outweighed visual impression. Wells at 1 uM, 10 uM, 0.3 uM and 1 uM replicate all showed extreme irregular nuclei with nuc_form_factor z -26 to -50, nuc_solidity z -36 to -56 and frac_large_nucleus z +19 to +23, and compare_to_reference_atlas was unanimous Microtubule destabilizers 2/2 then 4/4 with 9/10 k-neighbours at 0.905-0.919 similarity.\n\nThe main misleading signal was describe_phenotype suggesting Aurora kinase inhibitors from 4 fields, which kept Aurora at 0.18 then 0.07 despite contrary atlas votes. Post-unblinding lookup did not rescue this because ChEMBL had no curated mechanisms and literature searches returned only peripheral microtubule-actin papers and zero hits for direct colcemid mechanism reviews.\n\nThe decisive acquisition choice was testing a neighboring lower dose and a replicate rather than more high doses, proving onset at 0.3 uM and reproducibility at 1 uM and justifying raising Microtubule destabilizers from 0.72 to 0.90.",
  "what_was_right": [
   "Recognized extreme negative nuc_form_factor and nuc_solidity plus high frac_large_nucleus across e2, e3, e7, e8 as reproducible mitotic-failure signature",
   "Trusted unanimous NSC vote in e4 and e9 with 9/10 k-neighbours and 0.905-0.919 similarity over single microscopist Aurora call in e5",
   "Sampled 1 uM, then 10 uM, then 0.3 uM plus 1 uM replicate to demonstrate dose-consistent onset and reproducibility before submitting at p=0.90"
  ],
  "what_was_wrong": [
   "describe_phenotype e5 candidate Aurora kinase inhibitors inflated alternative hypothesis to 0.18 and was not discriminated visually at the time",
   "lookup_compound e12 gave no curated ChEMBL mechanisms and search_literature e13-e15 gave only peripheral papers or zero hits, so no independent literature confirmation was obtained",
   "Retained Eg5 inhibitors and Microtubule stabilizers at low probability from 1/10 neighbour and shared cytoskeletal features without a direct discriminating measurement like spindle morphology or tubulin polymer assay"
  ],
  "citations": [
